WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 133: Millard Fillmore and the Compromise of 1850: The Man from the Log Cabin

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.480
[Rebecca] Today, we step into the presidency of America's 13th chief executive, Millard Fillmore.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.160
[Rebecca] Fillmore is often overlooked by history or remembered as a punchline for obscure trivia.

5
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.120
[Rebecca] Yet Fillmore rose from deeper poverty than almost any president in American history.

6
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.1000
[Rebecca] An illiterate cloth apprentice who bought a dictionary and studied by candlelight.

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:36.640
[Rebecca] In 1850, when civil war threatened to tear the nation apart, Fillmore sacrificed his own political future to preserve the Union.

8
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

9
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:54.240
[George] In January 1800, in a squalid, drafty log cabin in the frozen wilderness of Summerhill in Cayuga County, New York, Millard Fillmore was born into crushing, destitute poverty.

10
00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:07.840
[George] His father, Nathaniel Fillmore, was a subsistence farmer who had been swindled by unscrupulous land speculators, losing what little land he possessed and reducing his family to tenant farming on rocky, barren clay soil.

11
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.600
[George] Young Millard spent his childhood performing grueling manual farm labor from dawn to dusk.

12
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:23.920
[George] There were virtually no schools in the frontier wilderness, and in the Fillmore cabin there were only three books: a King James Bible, a hymn book, and an outdated almanac.

13
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:35.920
[George] At age 14, desperate to reduce the number of mouths to feed, Millard's father bound the boy out as an indentured apprentice to a brutal cloth dresser and wool carter in Sparta, New York.

14
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.120
[George] For five grueling years, young Millard lived in a state of bitter semi-servitude.

15
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:54.560
[George] Enduring exhausting 12-hour shifts lifting heavy wet wool, handling harsh chemical dyes, and suffering under a tyrannical master who beat him with wooden clubs, starved him, and treated him worse than a draft beast.

16
00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:05.080
[George] When the master threatened him with an axe during a dispute over wages, Fillmore stood his ground with a woodsman's ferocity, threatening to cleave the man in two if he stepped forward.

17
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.200
[George] At age 18, refusing to accept a life of hopeless frontier ignorance.

18
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.400
[George] Fillmore made an extraordinary heroic decision.

19
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:24.120
[George] He pooled his meager pennies earned from extra firewood chopping, bought an unabridged English dictionary, and began teaching himself the structure of the English language word by word.

20
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.240
[George] Studying by the flickering light of burning pine knots after sixteen hours of labor.

21
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:41.080
[George] In 1819, at age 19, Fillmore scraped together $30 to buy out the remaining months of his indenture.

22
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:51.400
[George] Walked 100 miles on foot through rain and mud to Buffalo, New York, and began clerking in a local law office, determined to master the supreme law of the land.

23
00:02:51.400 --> 00:03:00.120
[George] Millard Fillmore's ascent from an illiterate apprentice to a respected jurist was a testament to the boundless possibilities of the American frontier.

24
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:06.760
[George] He was tall, strikingly handsome, impeccably dressed, and possessed unfailing, courteous manners.

25
00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.960
[George] Queen Victoria would later pronounce Fillmore the most handsome man she had ever seen.

26
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:24.680
[George] In Buffalo, Fillmore quickly became one of the most prominent trial lawyers in Western New York, founding the prestigious law firm of Fillmore, Hall and Haven, and helping establish the University of Buffalo.

27
00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:37.320
[George] His political mentor was Thurlow Weed, the master strategist of New York politics, who helped elect Fillmore to the New York State Assembly and later to four terms in the United States House of Representatives.

28
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:50.240
[George] In Congress, Fillmore served as the powerful chairman of the House Ways and Means Committee, drafting the landmark Whig Tariff of 1842 that revived American manufacturing after the Panic of 1837.

29
00:03:50.240 --> 00:04:00.400
[George] In 1848, to balance the presidential ticket with Southern slaveholder Zachary Taylor, the Whig Party chose Fillmore as their nominee for vice president.

30
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.760
[George] The illiterate boy from the Cayuga Log Cabin had reached the steps of the executive mansion.

31
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:14.320
[George] As vice president, Fillmore served as president of the Senate during the most titanic, explosive legislative session in American history.

32
00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.120
[George] The Great Senate Debates of 1850.

33
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:25.760
[George] For eight months, the Senate chamber was packed with breathless crowds watching the immortal trio, Henry Clay, Daniel Webster, and John C.

34
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.040
[George] Calhoun deliver their final, historic speeches on the fate of the American Union.

35
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:37.920
[George] Tensions were so volcanic that senators routinely carried loaded pistols and bowie knives onto the Senate floor.

36
00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:53.040
[George] During one fiery exchange, Senator Henry Foote of Mississippi drew a five-chambered revolver and aimed it directly at the chest of Missouri Senator Thomas Hart Benton in the center aisle, while Benton ripped open his shirt shouting, Let him fire.

37
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.240
[George] Stand out of the way and let the assassin fire.

38
00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:03.920
[George] Fillmore, presiding with calm, unflappable dignity, restored order by threatening to arrest any member who drew a weapon.

39
00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:14.160
[George] While Fillmore watched from the Dais, he realized that President Zachary Taylor's stubborn refusal to accept any compromise was pushing Southern states into immediate secession.

40
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:29.040
[George] Fillmore met privately with President Taylor in early July, informing him that if the Senate deadlocked on Clay's compromise bill, Fillmore would exercise his constitutional right as vice president to cast the tiebreaking vote in favor of the compromise.

41
00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.120
[George] Two days later, Zachary Taylor ate his fatal bowl of cherries and milk.

42
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:45.960
[George] At noon on Wednesday, July 10, 1850, in the hall of the House of Representatives, 50-year-old Millard Fillmore took the oath of office as the 13th President of the United States.

43
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.080
[George] The transition was instantaneous and complete.

44
00:05:49.080 --> 00:06:00.520
[George] Recognizing that Taylor's cabinet was adamantly opposed to legislative compromise, Fillmore accepted their resignations and constructed an entirely new cabinet composed of moderate unionists.

45
00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:05.160
[George] Fillmore appointed 70-year-old Daniel Webster as Secretary of State.

46
00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:16.600
[George] Webster was a giant of American law who had sacrificed his own standing in abolitionist Massachusetts by delivering his famous, 7th of March speech, pleading with the North to compromise.

47
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:22.360
[George] I speak today not as a Massachusetts man, nor as a Northern man, but as an American.

48
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.920
[George] I speak for the preservation of the Union.

49
00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:33.880
[George] Fillmore immediately sent a decisive message to the Governor of Texas, who had mobilized state militia to seize Santa Fe and eastern New Mexico.

50
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:47.320
[George] Fillmore warned Texas that New Mexico was under federal protection, ordered 750 federal reinforcements to Santa Fe, and warned that any armed Texan entering New Mexico would be met with federal artillery.

51
00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:55.000
[George] Fillmore showed the nation that while he championed legislative compromise, he would crush armed rebellion with relentless force.

52
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.720
[George] With Taylor's roadblock removed, 37-year-old Senator Stephen A.

53
00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:03.400
[George] Douglas of Illinois took charge of the legislative strategy.

54
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:11.000
[George] Douglas recognized that Henry Clay's massive omnibus bill had failed because Northern and Southern radicals voted against it together.

55
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:20.160
[George] Douglas unbundled the omnibus into five separate, standalone bills, allowing moderate Whigs and Democrats to form majorities on each measure.

56
00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:31.200
[George] Between September 9 and September 20, 1850, President Millard Fillmore signed all five bills into law, enacting the historic compromise of 1850.

57
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:40.400
[George] First, California was admitted to the Union as a free state, permanently giving free states a 16-15 majority in the United States Senate.

58
00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:49.440
[George] Second, the territories of New Mexico and Utah were organized under popular sovereignty, allowing future settlers to decide on slavery.

59
00:07:49.440 --> 00:08:00.560
[George] Third, Texas surrendered its disputed land claims to New Mexico, in exchange for the federal government assuming $10 million of Texas's pre annexation national debt.

60
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:10.080
[George] Fourth, the public slave trade, the horrific auctioning of human beings in sight of the Capitol Dome was permanently abolished in the District of Columbia.

61
00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:24.720
[George] And fifth, to appease the South, Congress enacted the new Fugitive Slave Act, which placed federal marshals and commissioners at the disposal of slave owners to capture suspected runaway slaves in northern free states without a jury trial.

62
00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:38.800
[George] While the Compromise of 1850 was celebrated across Washington with church bells, torchlight parades, and banquets hailing Fillmore as the savior of the Union, the Fugitive Slave Act ignited a moral firestorm across the North.

63
00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] The law denied accused runaways the right to testify in their own defense, imposed heavy fines and prison sentences on any citizen who refused to assist federal marshals in capturing escaped slaves, and paid federal commissioners $10 if they ruled a captive was a slave.

64
00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.720
[George] But only five dollars if they ruled him free.

65
00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:03.080
[George] Northern abolitionists erupted in incandescent, righteous fury.

66
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:06.040
[George] Ralph Waldo Emerson wrote bitterly in his journal.

67
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:11.240
[George] This filthy enactment was made in the 19th century by people who could read and write.

68
00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.920
[George] I will not obey it.

69
00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:23.800
[George] By God, Harriet Beecher Stowe was so morally galvanized by the passage of the act and Fillmore's signature that she sat down at her kitchen table in Maine to write Uncle Tom's Cabin.

70
00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:29.880
[George] The novel that awakened the conscience of the world and sold hundreds of thousands of copies across the globe.

71
00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.440
[George] In northern cities, peaceful citizens turned into defiant outlaws.

72
00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:49.400
[George] In Boston, Syracuse and Milwaukee, angry crowds of citizens stormed federal courthouses with axes, broke open jail doors, and whisked fugitive slaves away along the secret routes of the Underground Railroad to safety in Canada.

73
00:09:49.400 --> 00:10:01.000
[George] Vigilance committees were formed across New England to protect free black neighbors from Southern slave catchers, while church bells tolled in mournful protest whenever federal marshals appeared in town.

74
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:11.480
[George] Fillmore was personally opposed to slavery, but as president, he believed his sworn constitutional oath required him to enforce all federal laws without exception.

75
00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:25.240
[George] When mob resistance erupted in Boston over the arrest of fugitive slave Anthony Burns, Fillmore deployed federal troops, marines, and artillery to march Burns to a revenue cutter, enforcing the law at the point of bayonets.

76
00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:32.600
[George] Northern Whigs denounced Fillmore as a traitor to freedom, and his political standing in the North was permanently destroyed.

77
00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:38.040
[George] Millard Fillmore paid the ultimate political price for signing the Compromise of 1850.

78
00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:47.360
[George] When the Whig National Convention met in 1852, Northern delegates rejected their sitting president, nominating General Winfield Scott instead.

79
00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:54.080
[George] Fillmore accepted his rejection with quiet dignity, retiring from the White House in March 1853.

80
00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:59.520
[George] For generations, historians criticized Fillmore for signing the Fugitive Slave Act.

81
00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.240
[George] Yet modern historians view Fillmore's actions through a profound strategic lens.

82
00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:20.640
[George] In 1850, had civil war erupted, the South possessed seasoned military commanders, a unified political will and vast cotton wealth, while the North was ideologically fractured, militarily unprepared, and lacked a connected rail infrastructure.

83
00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:27.920
[George] By signing the Compromise of 1850, Millard Fillmore bought the American Union ten invaluable years of peace.

84
00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.840
[George] Between 1850 and 1860, the North underwent an explosive industrial revolution.

85
00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.440
[George] Over 20,000 miles of railroad tracks were laid.

86
00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:51.840
[George] Connecting the agricultural Midwest to Eastern factories, millions of European immigrants poured into northern cities, and a new political party was born, dedicated to stopping the expansion of slavery, the Republican Party.

87
00:11:51.840 --> 00:12:03.600
[George] When the Civil War finally exploded in 1861, the Union possessed the industrial might, rail networks, and manpower necessary to achieve total victory under Abraham Lincoln.

88
00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:15.600
[George] Millard Fillmore, the boy from the Cayuga Log Cabin, sacrificed his own reputation to preserve the nation, proving that the quiet courage of compromise can sometimes save a civilization.

89
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:25.760
[Rebecca] Millard Fillmore's willingness to sacrifice his political career for the Compromise of 1850 bought the American Union a crucial decade to prepare for the Civil War.

90
00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:42.920
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to Millard Fillmore's greatest foreign policy triumph, dispatching Commodore Matthew Perry and his terrifying black ships into Tokyo Bay in 1853, forcing open the closed kingdom of Japan and changing world history forever.

91
00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:47.320
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

